  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 102 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe long term forecast remains a tale of two halves. On the front\nhalf will be above average temperatures for November as a \nrelatively warm and dry airmass creeps into the area on the \nbeginning of the work week. The back half will feature a system \nmoving across the eastern half the US that may bring some rain but\nseems highly likely to bring a cool down to more seasonable \ntemperatures for mid-November.\n\nStarting on the front end Monday, the main polar jet should be to\nthe north of the CWA despite a strong upper level low rotating \nacross the NE. The air mass over the CWA will remain dry, but \nrepeated rounds of solar insolation each day should lead to \nwarming into the upper 60s to 70s for most by the late afternoon. \nThis is a good 5 to 10 degrees above average for daytime highs \nthis time of year. Flow remains to the north of the CWA through \nWednesday with only a cutoff low over Texas ejecting into the area\nbut bringing no precip with it. Forecast confidence is high \nthrough this time period.\n\nMoving into Thursday, forecast confidence begins to lower. \nMultiple waves look to interact over the eastern coast, and the \ndetails of those interactions including how and where they phase \nwill have big impacts on the forecast. EOF analysis of the 500 mb \nheights shows highest variance along the western half of the \ntrough digging into the east coast, which isn't surprising when \nlooking at some of the individual members. Several shortwaves are \nembedded in the larger wave here, and the details of exactly where\nthose will be this far out is a challenging model problem to say \nthe least. K-mean cluster analysis of the grand ensemble (US, \nCanada, and Euro) shows this challenge beautifully, with each of \nthe ensembles contributing members to each cluster. Some clusters \nare less phased (think 06Z GFS) on the western side, leading to \ngreater chances of rainfall across the southeast with a surface \nlow that develops and taps into moisture further to the south. The\nothers show better phasing further east, which leads to less \nrainfall across the area. There has been a trend towards the less \nphased solution across the ensemble members, so the increase in \nPoPs for Thursday reflects that.\n\nWhat is a bit more certain within the forecast on and after \nThursday is that a cold front should sweep through the area, \nregardless of what the outcome of the above is. Highs on Friday \nwill be in the 50s and 60s for most - much more seasonable. Lows \nwill drop into the 30s by Friday night, with some areas across \nnorth Georgia seeing subfreezing temperatures. Parts of the \nmountains and some of the valleys are forecast to have a hard \nfreeze with temperatures dropping into the mid 20s. The growing \nseason has mostly wrapped up, but those with vulnerable plants \nwill need to get them indoors (or sheltered in some way).",
